WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.560
Bruce Banner and Natasha Romanoff&nbsp;
have a complicated history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:07.840
From that scary chase scene to&nbsp;
that infamous heart-to-heart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:12.960
here are the biggest Black Widow and Hulk&nbsp;
scenes in the MCU ranked from best to worst.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:19.200
The best scene between Natasha Romanoff and Bruce&nbsp;
Banner just might be their first. It comes in "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:23.600
Avengers," when Natasha has been tasked with&nbsp;
recruiting Bruce to the team. At the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:27.920
Bruce is on the lam, living in Kolkata and&nbsp;
working as a medical doctor. He shows up at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:32.560
what he believes to be the home of a patient, but&nbsp;
instead, he finds Agent Romanoff waiting for him.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:37.360
Bruce is immediately on edge, and he asks if&nbsp;
the building is surrounded. But Widow is calm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:41.920
and casual, assuring him that they're alone. She&nbsp;
tells Bruce that she's come to recruit him for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:47.200
scientific expertise. Smiling nervously, Bruce&nbsp;
muses that perhaps she's come to lock him up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:51.040
which Widow denies. Finally, Bruce&nbsp;
slams down his fists and shouts,

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:52.800
"Stop lying to me!"

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:59.280
At this, Natasha becomes deadly serious and draws&nbsp;
a hidden gun. It's now clear that Natasha has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:04.720
absolutely terrified of Bruce this entire time&nbsp;
but was hiding it like a champ, something further&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:08.960
underlined by the reveal that S.H.I.E.L.D. agents&nbsp;
had the building surrounded the entire time.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:12.080
It's a tense and beautifully&nbsp;
constructed scene that also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:17.120
manages to quickly teach us everything we need to&nbsp;
know about these two characters moving forward.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:22.560
Bruce is trying to achieve balance between his&nbsp;
two halves and doesn't want to be caged. Natasha&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:28.160
is excellent at hiding her feelings, and she's&nbsp;
far deadlier than she seems. On top of all that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:33.200
it's Mark Ruffalo's first real scene playing&nbsp;
Banner, and he totally knocks it out of the park.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.680
Another of our favorite moments between Hulk&nbsp;
and Widow comes at the end of "Avengers:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:42.400
Age of Ultron," in a scene that's arguably the&nbsp;
final resolution of their romantic subplot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:46.240
though we didn't know it at the time. After&nbsp;
defeating Ultron, the Avengers are fleeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:50.240
the floating chunk of Sokovia where they&nbsp;
took their stand against the killer robot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:54.400
Although most of the team, including Black&nbsp;
Widow, escaped aboard a S.H.I.E.L.D. Helicarrier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:59.200
the Hulk opted for a different way off the&nbsp;
island, flying away in a stolen Quinjet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:04.400
As he flies further and further away from the rest&nbsp;
of the team, Natasha pops up with a video message.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:09.280
With visible concern and longing on her face,&nbsp;
she pleads with him to turn the plane around.

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:15.040
Instead, Hulk terminates the message and sits in&nbsp;
silence as the Quinjet flies off into the unknown.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:17.760
It's a beautiful little scene&nbsp;
that we enjoy, in large part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:22.000
because of its simplicity. Bruce never wanted&nbsp;
to be an Avenger in the first place, and now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:26.960
he has a chance to disappear forever. His only&nbsp;
regret is that he won't get to see Nat again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:32.000
and all of this is clear on both of their&nbsp;
faces, without either of them saying a word.

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:37.920
Another Hulk and Black Widow scene that we&nbsp;
simply adore comes early in "Age of Ultron,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:42.320
as the Avengers celebrate victory over HYDRA&nbsp;
back at Stark Tower. Natasha is pouring some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:46.400
drinks behind the bar, and Bruce walks up&nbsp;
to her, and things get a little flirty.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:51.840
It begins with pleasant banter, as Bruce uses&nbsp;
an old movie lean to get Natasha to play along.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:56.560
As their flirtation continues, though, Natasha&nbsp;
essentially confesses that she has feelings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:00.720
for Bruce and tries to get a sense of&nbsp;
whether or not he feels the same way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:05.520
Since Bruce is a huge dork, though, he's&nbsp;
less skilled at this sort of banter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:08.400
and he fails to give Nat the&nbsp;
answer she's looking for.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:12.880
This moment is a big shift in their relationship,&nbsp;
when their simmering romance officially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:18.880
transitions from subtext to text. Though this&nbsp;
romantic pairing took many audiences by surprise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:23.840
during their initial viewing of this film, it's&nbsp;
a genuinely great little interaction. The two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:29.200
have some real chemistry, and it's nice to see&nbsp;
two somber characters having a little fun. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:34.880
it's a little thing, but we love the fact that&nbsp;
Widow, being a veteran superspy, is way better at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:40.080
flirty roleplaying than the awkward and uptight&nbsp;
Banner. All in all, this scene is a delight.

00:03:41.102 --> 00:03:45.280
When Loki's forces launch a surprise attack&nbsp;
against the S.H.I.E.L.D. Helicarrier in "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:50.640
Avengers," an explosion sends Bruce and Natasha&nbsp;
tumbling down into the aircraft's lower decks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:54.000
Natasha finds her leg pinned&nbsp;
under some debris, and Bruce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.880
overwhelmed by his stressful surroundings,&nbsp;
starts to turn green. By the time Natasha&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:04.480
frees herself and starts limping away, Bruce has&nbsp;
become the Hulk. From there, a tense chase scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:10.160
ensues as an injured Widow struggles to run and&nbsp;
hide from a monster that used to be her ally.

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:14.560
This little horror interlude is a really&nbsp;
cool scene, and it's a nice tonal contrast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:19.920
with Bruce and Natasha's usual flirtations.&nbsp;
That being said, we don't love the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:23.760
during this sequence in which all the&nbsp;
rest of Avengers get to do cool stuff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:28.320
the lone female member of the team is relegated&nbsp;
to getting injured, running away from a monster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:32.800
and then spending the rest of the sequence&nbsp;
cowering in a storage room before finally getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:38.080
rescued by Thor. After losing a few points for&nbsp;
that, this scene is still pretty great overall.

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:44.240
After their tearful goodbye at the end of "Age&nbsp;
of Ultron," Hulk and Widow's romantic subplot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:48.640
is more or less dropped from the series moving&nbsp;
forward. There is, however, a little nod to their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:53.040
past entanglement in "Avengers: Infinity War,"&nbsp;
when the team is re-assembling in order to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:57.680
a stand against Thanos. After spending several&nbsp;
years as a fugitive, when Black Widow finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:02.480
rejoins the team, she finds an unexpected&nbsp;
person waiting for her back at Avengers HQ.

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:04.880
During Bruce and Natasha's brief interaction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:08.560
it's immediately clear that the pair still&nbsp;
have a great deal of affection for one another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:13.280
but it's also clear that neither one is entirely&nbsp;
sure of where they stand or what they want from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:18.800
each other. The two never get to have another&nbsp;
big heart-to-heart, at least not one that we see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:23.120
and they spend the rest of the film fighting&nbsp;
against Thanos, rather than patching things up.

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:27.600
We wish this scene could be a bit longer&nbsp;
and that it gave us a resolution to this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:32.800
particular plotline, but "Infinity War" is such&nbsp;
a dense film that we imagine the filmmakers just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:37.360
didn't have time for it. That being said,&nbsp;
what we do get here is still pretty great,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:41.520
so this awkwardly sweet little scene stands&nbsp;
out among the Bruce and Natasha moments.

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:46.080
Here's a scene we have mixed feelings&nbsp;
about from "Age of Ultron." Natasha has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:50.640
been captured by Ultron, and Bruce arrives&nbsp;
to save her. He frees her from her jail cell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:54.240
and tries to convince her to leave with&nbsp;
him, but she wants to stay and fight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:57.840
We cut away for a bit and see that&nbsp;
Ultron has begun his final plan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:01.920
to levitate a section of Sokovia up into the&nbsp;
sky. When we return to Bruce and Natasha,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:06.640
he tries again to convince her to leave with&nbsp;
renewed vigor. Instead, Natasha does this:

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:07.440
"I adore you."

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:12.560
After the kiss, she pushes him into&nbsp;
a pit. A moment later, the Hulk leaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:16.800
up from the abyss below and lands in front&nbsp;
of Natasha, grinning and ready for action.

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:20.800
This scene isn't higher on our list because&nbsp;
it's an example of a time when the writers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:25.440
want to have it both ways. They want to show the&nbsp;
audience that after everything he's gone through,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:30.720
Bruce is genuinely traumatized and he no longer&nbsp;
wants to be the Hulk. At the same time, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:36.080
for the climax of their big superhero movie, they&nbsp;
want the good guy version of Hulk to be involved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:40.720
punching robots and stuff. Their way of&nbsp;
accomplishing both these goals is to have Natasha&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:46.720
non-consensually transform an unwilling Bruce.&nbsp;
It's fine, but it's also sort of a cheap "gotcha"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:51.200
twist, and it's played entirely for laughs in a&nbsp;
way that doesn't really acknowledge that this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:56.080
a genuinely awful thing for Nat to do to Bruce,&nbsp;
who really doesn't want to be the Hulk anymore.

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:00.800
After the team breaks through the defenses of&nbsp;
a HYDRA base, it's time for the Avengers to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:05.360
reassemble and head home. However, since the&nbsp;
Hulk is still rampaging around in the woods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:09.520
Natasha is sent out to calm him down. And&nbsp;
it's here that we get to see the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:13.520
appearance of the team's new technique&nbsp;
for de-Hulking the Hulk — the lullaby.

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:19.600
"Hey, big guy, sun's getting real low."

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:24.720
With a gentle caress of the Hulk's palm and&nbsp;
forearm after uttering this code phrase,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:29.040
Natasha seems to immediately calm&nbsp;
the Hulk down. Then, after a moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:33.920
the big guy backs away and collapses to the&nbsp;
ground, transforming back into a shirtless Banner.

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:37.920
Honestly, we can't really decide how we feel&nbsp;
about this scene. It's sort of a cool idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:42.720
that Widow has the ability to help calm down the&nbsp;
Hulk and get him to transform back into Banner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:47.680
but the whole scene has a sort of uncomfortable&nbsp;
energy to it that just doesn't work for us. We're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:50.560
not really sure what director Joss&nbsp;
Whedon was thinking with this one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:56.320
but if he was going for sexy, he very much&nbsp;
missed the mark. It seems like perhaps even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.320 --> 00:08:01.360
some of the other folks at Marvel also weren't&nbsp;
super big fans of this somewhat awkward scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:04.320
as it was later turned into a&nbsp;
punchline in "Thor: Ragnarok."

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:09.200
Despite their long history, Black&nbsp;
Widow and the Hulk share barely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:12.560
any screen time during their final&nbsp;
film together, "Avengers: Endgame."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:16.480
Natasha's storyline focuses much more&nbsp;
on her relationship with Hawkeye,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:21.120
and Bruce's time is split between reconnecting&nbsp;
with Thor and doing science stuff. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:25.360
being said, the pair do have one brief&nbsp;
interaction together early on in the film.

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:29.440
When Natasha, Steve, and Scott meet Bruce at&nbsp;
a diner, we learn that the big guy has now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:34.960
merged his two halves, Hulk and Banner, into a&nbsp;
single being. Banner explains that one reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:40.240
he decided to do this was because he personally&nbsp;
blames himself for the team's loss to Thanos.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:44.480
There's no flirting, but Natasha&nbsp;
does make some time to comfort Bruce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:49.200
and to compliment him when he worries he might&nbsp;
not have the expertise to figure out time travel:

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:54.480
"Well, you pulled this off. I remember a time&nbsp;
when that seemed pretty impossible, too."

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:58.560
The scene is more of a group moment, but it's&nbsp;
worth mentioning because believe it or not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:02.320
it's the last time that we get to see&nbsp;
Hulk and Widow have a real conversation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:05.600
It's a perfectly fine scene. The&nbsp;
only reason we're ranking it so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:11.040
low is because of how anticlimactic this ends&nbsp;
up being as the pair's last true interaction.

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:16.480
After a loss to Ultron that included an&nbsp;
accidental Hulk rampage, the team decides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:21.760
to lay low at Hawkeye's rural family home. While&nbsp;
they're there, Natasha decides to make her play.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:26.240
She asks Bruce to run away with her, but he&nbsp;
refuses, saying she's out of her mind. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:30.800
reasoning? He feels that being the Hulk means&nbsp;
he can't have a normal relationship with her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:34.720
and he can't have kids. She responds&nbsp;
by telling him she can't either,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:37.920
and that the Red Room that trained&nbsp;
her also had her sterilized.

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:44.160
"It's efficient. One less thing to worry about."

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:49.040
Perhaps there's a version of this scene&nbsp;
that could've worked. We'd love to see more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:53.360
of Widow and Hulk talking about how they&nbsp;
both feel like monsters who don't fit in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:57.680
That whole dynamic is the main reason why&nbsp;
they work so well as a pairing. But in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:02.160
specific execution, the writers tried to&nbsp;
convey this idea in a truly tasteless way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:06.720
by having both of them lament their&nbsp;
infertility. Presumably without realizing it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:10.960
the scene basically implies that people who can't&nbsp;
have kids are unfit to be in a relationship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.960 --> 00:10:15.360
and that they're, well, monsters. Even&nbsp;
if it wasn't the intended message,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:21.440
this complete catastrophe of a scene clearly&nbsp;
gets the bottom spot on our list, with a bullet.

